  "science_checks": [
    {
      "id": "S1",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0181#9", "p0182#1"],
      "topic": "Interaction energy and fixed external field",
      "judgment": "Potential energy is assigned to the interaction system, not an intrinsic store of an isolated probe charge. The field/source distribution is static and probe disturbance negligible; radiation, self-field effects and medium changes are expressly neglected. Electric work is not equated with the work of every force."
    },
    {
      "id": "S2",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0181#10"],
      "topic": "Atomic, chemical and nuclear energy",
      "judgment": "The modern passage explicitly rejects the source's unqualified attribution of reactor heat to depletion of nuclear electrostatic energy. It includes strong-interaction nuclear binding and initial/final rest-energy differences, while distinguishing quantum microscopic binding from a macroscopic electrostatic formula. It supplies no invented nuclear measurements or new equations."
    },
    {
      "id": "S3",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0182#2", "p0182#3", "p0182#4"],
      "topic": "Plate orientation, coordinate and positive work",
      "judgment": "The left negative B and right positive D are preserved as textual source identities. Distance increases from the left plate toward the right, opposite the field vector, while E in the scalar formula is magnitude. For the expressly positive q and d2<d1, leftward motion gives positive electric work. The large parallel-plate central-region approximation and fixed source are explicit."
    },
    {
      "id": "S4",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0182#9", "p0183#1"],
      "topic": "Path independence and signed projection",
      "judgment": "The staircase argument sums signed field-parallel displacement rather than unsigned path length; transverse segments do no electric work in the uniform model. Reversals cancel in the algebraic projection. The staircase is expressly a calculation construction, not an asserted particle trajectory."
    },
    {
      "id": "S5",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0183#2", "p0183#3", "p0183#4", "p0183#5"],
      "topic": "Work minus energy change and charge signs",
      "judgment": "With the negative plate chosen as zero, EP=qEd yields A=qE(d1-d2)=-Delta EP. The sign relation is valid for signed q in the stated uniform fixed external field; the earlier positive-work example is explicitly restricted to positive q. Negative q reverses the work and force signs without changing the coordinate or zero convention. The units of qEd are energy, not force or potential."
    },
    {
      "id": "S6",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0183#6"],
      "topic": "Kinetic energy and deceleration",
      "judgment": "Conversion of decreasing potential energy into particle kinetic energy is conditional on only the electric force doing work and ignored radiation/other transfers. Positive-charge motion against the field increases potential energy and reduces kinetic energy under those same assumptions. Deceleration is not an assertion of immediate stopping, and no hazardous apparatus procedure is provided."
    },
    {
      "id": "S7",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0183#7", "p0183#8"],
      "topic": "Closed-path work and repeated endpoint",
      "judgment": "Zero closed-path electric work is scoped to a static field and unchanged source/probe model. Both occurrences of the initial energy in the printed formula are retained, correctly representing return to the same endpoint. The statement is not generalized to induced electric fields."
    },
    {
      "id": "S8",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0183#9", "p0183#10", "p0183#11"],
      "topic": "Arbitrary energy reference",
      "judgment": "The zero at d0 gives EP=qEd-qEd0, a position-independent shift for the same charge and source model. Forces and energy differences remain unchanged. The modern text qualifies the source's blanket claim that energy itself has no physical meaning by explaining reference dependence instead."
    },
    {
      "id": "S9",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_refs": ["p0184#1"],
      "topic": "Gravity analogy and negative energy",
      "judgment": "The gravity analogy is restricted to the near-surface model. Signed electric charge is distinguished from mass. Under the stated negative-plate zero and nonnegative distance, negative q gives negative interaction potential energy away from the zero, while zero remains zero. This is not confused with negative kinetic energy or failure of conservation."
    }
  ],
